  "honest_note": "T3 and T9 are 15/15 observations and are NOT promoted. Every other claim that depends on T3 is marked '손증명 (given T3)' rather than 손증명.",
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "claim": "R1 target orbit is O* = orbit 1",
      "id": "T1",
      "note": "follows from chaining plus T5, but chaining is itself unproved, so this is not independent. See RR_SAME_COMPONENT_CHAINING_LONG.md.",
      "status": "bounded observation (15/15)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "the hub completer targets (orbit 1, phase 4)",
      "id": "T2",
      "proof": "at ell=4 the abandonment's rotation run visits hex0 positions 0..4 and the joint leaves hex0, so position 5 is the UNIQUE unvisited hex0 position. A rotation run can only move inside the current hexagon, so hex0 can be re-entered only as a joint target; the completer is by definition the first such edge, and it cannot revisit, so its target is hex0 position 5. By (H1) that position is (orbit 1, phase 4).",
      "status": "손증명"
    },
    {
      "claim": "R2 fires on the very next macro-edge after C (tail = 0)",
      "id": "T3",
      "note": "the edge after C is forced to ell=0 (T4a), but all four joints are legal there and three of them are not R. Legality alone does not force the next edge to be R2.",
      "status": "bounded observation (15/15) -- NOT proved"
    },
    {
      "claim": "the macro-edge after C has rotation run ell = 0",
      "id": "T4a",
      "proof": "after C the walk stands on hex0 position 5; a weight-1 rotation would move to hex0 position 0, which is visited from the very first state, so exact.extend returns None. Hence ell = 0.",
      "status": "손증명"
    },
    {
      "claim": "if the edge after C is an R, that R is rot^0;w3:120",
      "id": "T4b",
      "proof": "at the post-C endpoint exactly one of the four joints is an R event: w2:10 is Z2, w3:201 and w3:210 are Z3 (fresh openings), and w3:120 is the only R. Combined with T4a the label is rot^0;w3:120.",
      "status": "손증명 (given T4a)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "R2 source orbit is orbit 1",
      "id": "T5",
      "proof": "by T2 the walk stands at (orbit 1, phase 4) when the next edge fires.",
      "status": "손증명 (given T3)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "R2 target orbit is the initial orbit 0",
      "id": "T6",
      "proof": "computed directly: rot^0;w3:120 from (1,4) lands in orbit 0.",
      "status": "손증명 (given T3, T4b)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Phi = 0 at the R2 boundary",
      "id": "T7",
      "proof": "Phi(initial) = 6. By (H2) a macro-edge with rotation run ell changes Phi by ell-5. The word is A_4 (ell=4, dPhi=-1), then P_core edges all at ell=5 (dPhi=0 each), then C at ell=5 (dPhi=0), then R2 at ell=0 (dPhi=-5). Total: 6 - 1 - 5 = 0. The preparation length cancels exactly, which is why short and long preparations agree.",
      "status": "손증명"
    },
    {
      "claim": "the R2 boundary is same-component",
      "id": "T8",
      "proof": "orbit 0 has a port at hex0 position 0, visited in the initial state; by T2 the completer visits hex0 position 5, a port of orbit 1. Both orbits are therefore incident to hexagon 0 and lie in one component. R2's source orbit is 1 (T5) and its target orbit is 0 (T6), so the same-component test passes automatically -- it is not a constraint at ell=4 once C has fired.",
      "status": "손증명 (given T3, T4b)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "chaining (R1 target orbit = R2 source orbit)",
      "id": "T9",
      "note": "see RR_SAME_COMPONENT_CHAINING_LONG.md for how far the argument gets.",
      "status": "bounded observation (15/15) -- NOT proved"
    }
  ],
